@node Mes internals
@chapter Mes internals

@menu
* Overview::                    Overview of the Scheme interpreter.
* Compilation::                 Compiling to bytecode.
* Bytecode Interpreter::        Interpreting bytecode.
* Boot Process::                How the Scheme interpreter is started.

@end menu

@node Overview
@section Overview

@cindex interpreter
Mes is a very simple Scheme interpreter.

Prior to version 0.28.0 it was a tree-walking interpreter but it was
rewritten as a bytecode interpreter for the 0.28.0 release.

Being a bytecode interpreter separates the stages of the interpreter
more clearly.  This gives us the chance to introduce small optimizations
and also make sure our code is as tight as possible in memory, which
results in better performance.  @footnote{In the first iteration to the
bytecode interpreter performance was improved by a 40%.}

The interpreter takes top-level expressions one by one, macro expands
them, compiles them and runs them.  This means compilation time is
interleaved with evaluation time.

@node Compilation
@section Compilation

The compilation unit of Mes is the procedure.  Each procedure is
compiled to a @dfn{template}, a data structure that holds the code
(bytecode) of the procedure, its literals, and some extra metadata.
There's no global literal pool: all literals are stored in the
template of the procedure they appear in.

Top-level expressions are wrapped in procedures and compiled to
templates, too.  Procedures that contain procedures compile the inner
procedures to templates and treat them as literals of the outer
procedure.

Templates are wrapped in closures, and are not accessible to the user
directly.  A @dfn{closure} is a template in a particular environment.
That is, it contains both the template and the variables it captured
from the environment where it was instantiated.  If two closures come
from the same function definition, the code is shared: they use the same
template.

If you want to see the bytecode of a closure, you can use the
@code{procedure-disassemble} procedure.

@deffn {Procedure} procedure-disassemble proc

Display the disassembly of the given procedure.  Returns
@code{*unspecified*}.

@itemize
@item
@var{proc}: Procedure to disassemble.
@end itemize
@end deffn

The following example shows a few interesting features.

@example
@verbatim
mes@(guile-user)> (procedure-disassemble (lambda (a) (+ 1 a)))
[sexp=(procedure-disassemble (lambda (a) (+ 1 a)))]
  ────────────────────────── 0x7f1ad4d04798
  - name: anonymous#2585
  - arity: 1
  - source: (core:lambda (a) (+ 1 a))
       0:	0x04          OP_LITERAL
       1:	0xaa                   0 1
       2:	0x00                   .
       3:	0x00                   .
       4:	0x09        OP_REF_LOCAL
       5:	0xaa                   0 a
       6:	0x00                   .
       7:	0x00                   .
       8:	0x0b          OP_RESOLVE
       9:	0x04          OP_LITERAL
      10:	0x01                   1 #<binding +: #<variable #<builtin +>>>
      11:	0x00                   .
      12:	0x0b          OP_RESOLVE
      13:	0x10        OP_TAIL_CALL
      14:	0x02                   2 2
      15:	0x00                   .
      16:	0x11           OP_RETURN
@end verbatim
@end example

First, one can see how the @code{+} symbol is already bound to a builtin
showing that all global variables in the code are resolved at compile
time.  Similarly, the symbol @code{a} is recognized as a local variable
and reduced to an index (0 in this case) for faster lookups.

Second, what the template stores as the @code{source} is not the original
code, but the macro expanded version, which is reduced to a simplified
Scheme, not the one that one would write manually.  That separation
between human-written Scheme and the compiler input makes the code easier
to process by the compiler.

This is the list of special syntax the compiler accepts:

@itemize
@item @code{(core:gc)}
@item @code{(core:set-mark! mark)}
@item @code{(core:ref-mark)}
@item @code{(core:apply proc args)}
@item @code{(core:begin exp ...)}
@item @code{(core:call/cc handler)}
@item @code{(core:define name value)}
@item @code{(core:if condition true-case false-case)}
@item @code{(core:lambda formals body ...)}
@item @code{(core:quote exp)}
@item @code{(core:set! binding|name value)}
@end itemize

This simplified Scheme is compiled down to a representation that matches
its meaning but is bytecode for a stack-based virtual machine.

@node Bytecode Interpreter
@section Bytecode Interpreter

In Mes, all values are heap allocated, but they are arranged in a stack.
Local variables, procedure arguments, temporary values@enddots{}
Everything is put on the stack.

The function @code{eval_compiled} in the file @file{src/eval-apply.c}
evaluates the code in the virtual machine.  It is a big loop that has
minimal internal state, mostly the @abbr{pc, program counter}, the
@abbr{fp, frame pointer}, the current procedure, and the current mark.
The loop increases the program counter, reads the current instruction,
evaluates it, and repeats.

When a procedure is called, it is set as the @emph{current procedure},
the @abbr{pc, program counter} is set to 0 and the loop continues, this
time over the code of the new @emph{current procedure}.

In order to be able to return to the caller, a @emph{frame} is pushed to
the stack.  This records the @abbr{pc, program counter} of the caller
procedure and the @abbr{fp, frame pointer}, which is the (stack-) index
of the caller frame.  With that, all the frames are connected to one
another by the @abbr{fp, frame pointer}.

When returning from a procedure, the @emph{frame} is read from the
@emph{fp} index on the stack. That is used to reset the virtual-machine
state to the one it had prior to the call, and the program continues its
execution from there, with the return value of the just resolved call on
the top of the stack.

As required by Scheme, the virtual machine also supports tail call
elimination.  The compiler recognizes when a procedure is called in tail
position, and emits special instructions to avoid pushing the current
procedure's frame onto the stack before making the new call.

This is the full list of operations the virtual machine supports.  All
arguments are 16-bit unsigned values, and they are aligned in memory by
adding an empty byte between the instruction and the argument when
needed.

@table @code

@item OP_UNSPECIFIED
Push @code{*unspecified*} to the stack.

@item OP_NIL
Push @code{()} to the stack.

@item OP_FALSE
Push @code{#f} to the stack.

@item OP_TRUE
Push @code{#t} to the stack.

@item OP_LITERAL, @var{ID}
Push the literal named @var{ID} to the stack.

@item OP_DROP
Drop the top of the stack.

@item OP_BRANCH, @var{TARGET}
Pop the top of the stack, if @code{#f} set the program counter to
@var{TARGET}.

@item OP_JUMP, @var{TARGET}
Set the program counter to @code{TARGET}.

@item OP_SET
Pop a location and a value from the stack and set the location to the
value.  Push @code{*unspecified*}.

@item OP_REF_LOCAL, @var{ID}
Push the local named @var{ID} to the stack.

@item OP_REF_CLOSURE, @var{ID}
Push the capture named @var{ID} to the stack.

@item OP_RESOLVE
Replace a global binding on the top of the stack with its current value.

@item OP_CLOSURE, @var{ARGNO}
Pop @var{ARGNO} values from the stack followed by a template and push a
closure formed with them back to the stack.

@item OP_CC, @var{TARGET}
Push a continuation to the stack that restores virtual machine state to
the current one but with the program counter set to @var{TARGET}.

@item OP_CALL, @var{ARGNO}
Pop a procedure (a closure, a builtin, or a continuation) and
@var{ARGNO} arguments from the stack and perform a procedure call.

@item OP_TAIL_CALL, @var{ARGNO}
Same as @code{OP_CALL} but performing a tail call.

@item OP_RETURN
Pop a value from the stack and replace the current frame by it
effectively returning to the caller procedure.  If there's no caller
procedure, stop evaluation and return the value.

@item OP_APPLY
Pop an argument list and a procedure to call from the stack, and then
call the procedure with those arguments.

@item OP_TAIL_APPLY
Same as @code{OP_APPLY} but performing a tail call.

@item OP_REF_MARK
Push the current mark to the stack.

@item OP_SET_MARK
Pop a value from the stack and set the current mark to it.

@item OP_GC
Perform garbage collection.

@item OP_UNREACHABLE
This is the default value of each byte and corresponds to @code{0xAA}.
Evaluating it produces an error.

@end table

@node Boot Process
@section Boot Process

When Mes is started it first sets everything up so the user program can
be run.  This is what we call the @dfn{boot process}.  Mes starts
without any macro expander; a very simple C loop drives program
execution.  This loop reads expressions from the initial boot file one
by one, compiling and executing them as it goes.

@cindex expander, macro expander
The initial boot file, @file{mes/module/mes/boot.scm}, defines a simple
Lisp-style macro-expander @footnote{In the sense that it does nothing to
help macro writers create hygienic macros.} written in Scheme.  Once
ready, the macro expander takes control of the program: it reads
expressions, expands them, compiles them, and then evaluates them.

From there, the rest of the boot process proceeds.  This is where many
basic procedures are defined and the module system is booted.  When this
is finished, control is finally transferred either to a user program or
the @abbr{REPL, read-eval-print loop}.

